// Pathfinder Run custom pedestrian model.
//
// Implements the static path-type weighting from
// docs/running-app-architecture.md §5, point 1's first bullet: prefer
// dedicated pedestrian infrastructure, penalize (not exclude) unprotected
// major roads, exclude anything without foot access.
//
// Deliberately NOT included here (separate, later work):
// - Edge-reuse penalty (app-level logic in the route generator, not a
//   GraphHopper profile concern)
// - The live closures penalty (per-request only -- see generate_loop.py's
//   fetch_return_leg/fetch_outbound_leg -- because it changes request to
//   request)
//
// Greenness/park-proximity (§5 point 1's second bullet) is REMOVED as of
// the full-Ontario migration, not just never-added -- it was here as an
// `in_greenspace` rule (see git history for the exact form and its
// verification) sourced from data/greenspace/greenspace.geojson via
// config.yml's `custom_areas.directory`, GraphHopper's STATIC per-import
// mechanism. That worked fine at city scale (Waterloo Region, Guelph) and
// was even confirmed CH/speed-mode compatible there. At province scale it
// is the confirmed root cause of /route being unusably slow in flexible
// mode (ch.disable=true, required by every per-request custom_model
// mechanism this app uses: edge-reuse, closures, the long-way buffer fix):
// `in_greenspace` is a live per-edge point-in-polygon lookup against
// 315,762 greenspace polygons, evaluated for every edge weight computation
// flexible mode has to make without CH's precomputed shortcuts to fall
// back on. Disambiguated directly, not inferred: an identical test query
// against a scratch Ontario graph with this exact rule stripped out
// completed in 49ms with LM prep enabled, versus 22-56s for the same query
// against the real graph with it in place -- everything else held equal.
//
// A proper fix would bake an in_greenspace-equivalent value into the graph
// as a static encoded value at import time (so it costs nothing per query,
// the same way road_class/surface/etc. do today), which requires a custom
// Java TagParser + GraphHopper ImportRegistry extension -- confirmed via
// decompiling the stock graphhopper-web.jar that no config-level or
// OSM-tag-preprocessing path can do this (GraphHopper's encoded-value
// registry is a fixed, closed set of ~50 hardcoded names, no generic
// tag-to-encoded-value mechanism exists). Filed as a future item, not
// blocking: multi-day Java build-out (new build toolchain, new custom
// Application entry point, a spatial-indexed preprocessing pass), not
// something to do incidentally here. scripts/build_greenspace.py and
// data/greenspace-ontario/ are left in place, unused, for whenever that
// work happens -- the polygon data itself doesn't need rebuilding.
//
// Note: this replaces the stock model's `multiply_by: foot_priority` rather than
// layering on top of it. GraphHopper's built-in `foot_priority` (FootPriorityParser)
// already does its own road-class weighting AND reads the literal `sidewalk=*` tag
// on a way directly -- a signal not exposed as any encoded value queryable here, so
// this explicit model can't distinguish "primary road with a mapped sidewalk" from
// "primary road with none." Keeping both would double-penalize/boost the same
// categories unpredictably, so this is an explicit tradeoff: visible, tunable rules
// here, at the cost of that sidewalk-tag signal until it's reintroduced deliberately.
//
// TRUNK/MOTORWAY get their own (lower) tier rather than sharing PRIMARY/SECONDARY's:
// the stock model penalizes fast/limited-access roads far harder than ordinary
// arterials (REACH_DESTINATION = 0.1x vs BAD = 0.5x for primary/secondary), and this
// keeps that same margin rather than flattening it.
{
  "priority": [
    { "if": "!foot_access", "multiply_by": "0" },
    { "if": "road_class == PATH || road_class == FOOTWAY || road_class == PEDESTRIAN || road_class == LIVING_STREET", "multiply_by": "1.5" },
    { "else_if": "road_class == PRIMARY || road_class == SECONDARY", "multiply_by": "0.3" },
    { "else_if": "road_class == TRUNK || road_class == MOTORWAY", "multiply_by": "0.1" }
  ],
  "speed": [
    { "if": "true", "limit_to": "foot_average_speed" }
  ]
}
